Pyro patch 0027: modules parsed and compiled ahead of their first load, kept by the VM.

A module load parses its source twice (once for the module's record: imports, exports, features;
once to generate its top-level code) and JavaScriptCore's code cache only remembers the second, and
only until the cache evicts it. A `serve --fork` template knows the application's files before any
page loads them, and every page it forks loads the same text again.

`JSModuleSourcePrecompile(ctx, source, url, functions)` parses `source` as the module a load of it
from `url` would be and keeps, in a table of the VM's CodeCache keyed by the source text and its
URL, the module's unlinked code and what its record is made of (requested modules, import, export
and star export entries, features, top-level await). It returns false for a text that is no module.
The table is not subject to the code cache's eviction or age; `CodeCache::clear()`
(`VM::deleteAllCode`) drops it. With `functions`, the unlinked code of every function of the module
is generated too.

`JSModuleLoader::makeModule` builds the record of a source in the table from the kept entries
instead of parsing, and `CodeCache::getUnlinkedGlobalCodeBlock` hands out the kept code for its
module program. A source that is not in the table takes the paths it took before. A collection
walks the functions that have code only in a VM that jettisons unlinked code: no other VM has
anything to reconcile there, and with `functions` the walk is over every function of the table.

`JSContextGroupGetModulePrecompileStats` reports the table's size and how many loads it served
(records, code) or did not, and how many functions the VM compiled lazily.

